12. What I Would Tell Your Manager

You can explain it this way:

“The spike confirmed that automating the external certificate renewal lifecycle is technically feasible. We identified DigiCert CertCentral as the certificate renewal and retrieval component, TeamCity as the orchestration layer, and Fastly or NSX-ALB as the downstream deployment targets depending on where each certificate terminates.

We also found an existing NSX-ALB TeamCity certificate automation that we can reuse as an implementation pattern. It already handles CSR generation, PowerShell orchestration, parameterization, API authentication and NSX-ALB certificate upload. The existing workflow currently uses the internal SILAC SubCA, so the external implementation will replace that issuance portion with DigiCert API calls.

For certificates terminating through Fastly, we'll need a separate downstream Fastly deployment step. Domain validation is being treated separately and will remain owned by SysAdmin.

The main implementation work now is integrating DigiCert APIs, implementing the Fastly certificate update workflow, establishing certificate-to-platform mapping, and adding post-deployment TLS validation and failure handling. Based on the research, I believe we have enough information to close the spike and move into implementation.”

13. Bottom Line
What the spike proved

✅ DigiCert can provide the certificate renewal/retrieval automation layer.

✅ NSX-ALB certificate operations can be automated through APIs.

✅ Existing TeamCity automation provides a reusable implementation pattern.

✅ Fastly must be included for externally terminated domains.

✅ TeamCity can serve as the central orchestration layer.

What is intentionally outside this automation

Domain validation/pre-validation → SysAdmin ownership

What implementation now needs to build

DigiCert integration + Fastly integration + NSX-ALB integration where required + TLS validation + monitoring/error handling.
